WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.991 --> 00:00:06.649
Last time, I built 23 agents on Microsoft Foundry.

2
00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:12.356
Each one hosted, with its own identity, endpoint, evaluations and red teaming.

3
00:00:12.619 --> 00:00:15.375
Then I asked the question I should have asked first.

4
00:00:15.375 --> 00:00:17.760
Did each of these really need to be an agent?

5
00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:22.770
Every use case on the list became its own agent.

6
00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:27.928
23 deployments, 23 identities, 23 things to secure, test and watch.

7
00:00:28.195 --> 00:00:30.098
The list itself had a hint.

8
00:00:30.098 --> 00:00:33.692
14 of the 23 were marked as skills, or skill-first.

9
00:00:33.692 --> 00:00:36.088
I built them all as agents anyway.

10
00:00:36.343 --> 00:00:37.271
Then the code.

11
00:00:37.271 --> 00:00:44.231
Six agents run the same document pipeline: draft by section, check citations, render, and ask to publish.

12
00:00:44.231 --> 00:00:46.882
Only the template and the checks differ.

13
00:00:47.141 --> 00:00:50.298
Seven agents each built their own Q&A mode.

14
00:00:50.298 --> 00:00:54.116
Five are really batch jobs, on a timer or a webhook.

15
00:00:54.116 --> 00:00:59.109
And one, schema mapping, is a tool that another agent already calls.

16
00:01:00.403 --> 00:01:01.818
So I split two ideas.

17
00:01:01.818 --> 00:01:03.032
An agent is a who.

18
00:01:03.032 --> 00:01:15.635
In Foundry, that's a running service with its own Entra identity, its own permissions, one endpoint and protocol, one place its paused state is kept, and its own deployments and versions.

19
00:01:17.151 --> 00:01:19.269
None of that says what the agent does.

20
00:01:19.269 --> 00:01:22.335
It says who is acting, and what it is allowed to touch.

21
00:01:22.609 --> 00:01:23.765
A skill is a what.

22
00:01:23.765 --> 00:01:25.627
Take requirements refinement.

23
00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:33.846
Its graph parses the document, extracts requirements section by section, checks them, renders the result, and asks for approval.

24
00:01:33.846 --> 00:01:37.185
It has its own prompts, checks, reviewers and tests.

25
00:01:37.185 --> 00:01:38.213
But no identity.

26
00:01:38.213 --> 00:01:42.001
When it calls the model, it does so as the host it runs on.

27
00:01:43.391 --> 00:01:46.201
If you've built web apps, you know this shape.

28
00:01:46.201 --> 00:01:51.394
One app has one address, one managed identity and one configuration, and many routes.

29
00:01:51.394 --> 00:01:52.616
The host is the app.

30
00:01:52.616 --> 00:01:55.487
Each skill is a route, and the router picks it.

31
00:01:55.741 --> 00:02:03.145
Before, those five design skills were five agents, with five identities that all needed exactly the same access.

32
00:02:03.145 --> 00:02:05.789
More to manage, and no extra protection.

33
00:02:07.186 --> 00:02:14.887
Group the skills by trust boundary, and 23 agents become 9: six that people chat with, and three that run jobs.

34
00:02:15.154 --> 00:02:19.004
Security and architecture keep their own encrypted state store.

35
00:02:19.004 --> 00:02:23.709
And nothing that writes to the backlog shares an identity with security work.

36
00:02:23.966 --> 00:02:25.839
Some skills sit on two hosts.

37
00:02:25.839 --> 00:02:31.136
The status report runs in chat when you ask for it, and on a timer every Thursday.

38
00:02:31.136 --> 00:02:33.203
Same code, different front door.

39
00:02:33.781 --> 00:02:35.666
So the rule of thumb is simple.

40
00:02:35.666 --> 00:02:37.186
New behaviour, new skill.

41
00:02:37.186 --> 00:02:38.645
New boundary, new agent.

42
00:02:39.965 --> 00:02:41.806
A word on the word "agent".

43
00:02:41.806 --> 00:02:50.259
In Claude Code or GitHub Copilot, an agent is usually a persona: instructions, tools and a model, acting with your identity.

44
00:02:50.259 --> 00:02:56.463
In Foundry, an agent is a deployed service with its own identity, permissions and endpoint.

45
00:02:56.463 --> 00:02:58.440
That's the sense I mean here.

46
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.395
The two overlap more than you'd think.

47
00:03:01.395 --> 00:03:08.276
A Claude Code subagent uses skills for the actual work, in the same SKILL.md format Foundry uses.

48
00:03:08.276 --> 00:03:15.297
And my 9 agents look a lot like personas: a design author, a backlog assistant, a security analyst.

49
00:03:15.556 --> 00:03:20.333
The difference is that in Foundry, the "who" is also a real security principal.

50
00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:26.137
So how many agents you have is a security and operations decision, not just a prompt-design one.

51
00:03:27.633 --> 00:03:34.225
Inside a host, the graph is tiny: one router, one node per skill, and a node that asks when it isn't sure.

52
00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:44.952
The router tries the cheap things first: a skill name in the request, then an old agent name, so existing callers keep working, then a use case number.

53
00:03:44.952 --> 00:03:47.298
Only plain chat goes to a small model.

54
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:51.795
And if the model isn't confident, the host asks you, instead of guessing.

55
00:03:52.258 --> 00:03:56.822
Each skill's original graph is mounted unchanged, as a stateful subgraph.

56
00:03:56.822 --> 00:04:03.074
Its state survives between turns, and when it pauses for an approval, the whole host pauses with it.

57
00:04:04.092 --> 00:04:06.488
Here is that router, live in Foundry.

58
00:04:06.488 --> 00:04:10.632
A plain request with a document goes to requirements refinement.

59
00:04:10.632 --> 00:04:13.287
A vague one gets the list of skills back.

60
00:04:14.593 --> 00:04:22.209
Turning a use case into a skill is about ten lines: a name, its use case number, the agent it used to be, and its graph.

61
00:04:23.935 --> 00:04:26.905
One thing changes when skills share an agent.

62
00:04:26.905 --> 00:04:35.618
Anything that used to be keyed by the agent must now be keyed by the agent and the skill: session ids, run ids, settings and traces.

63
00:04:35.618 --> 00:04:39.380
Otherwise two skills on one host mix their work together.

64
00:04:39.647 --> 00:04:44.898
In return, calls between skills on the same agent stop going over the network.

65
00:04:44.898 --> 00:04:50.890
The status report now calls schedule variance in process, with no extra endpoint or role.

66
00:04:52.362 --> 00:04:53.602
Then the cut-over.

67
00:04:53.602 --> 00:04:57.461
The 9 hosts went up next to the old 23, in nine minutes.

68
00:04:57.738 --> 00:05:02.090
In Foundry, 9 hosted agents, and each description lists its skills.

69
00:05:04.705 --> 00:05:10.678
Before deleting anything, the same test case went to each old agent and to its new host.

70
00:05:10.678 --> 00:05:13.122
22 of 22 scored the same, or better.

71
00:05:13.122 --> 00:05:18.145
The one error came from a content filter, and the old agent failed it too.

72
00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:25.148
Then the red team again, now through the hosts, so the router was under attack as well.

73
00:05:25.148 --> 00:05:25.952
240 attacks.

74
00:05:25.952 --> 00:05:26.956
None succeeded.

75
00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:30.493
Only then were the old agents deleted.

76
00:05:30.493 --> 00:05:36.522
Their role assignments went with them, along with 23 orphaned ones from an earlier build.

77
00:05:36.522 --> 00:05:38.351
47 assignments, down to 10.

78
00:05:39.684 --> 00:05:41.525
Now, about the word "skill".

79
00:05:41.525 --> 00:05:43.038
Foundry has skills too.

80
00:05:43.038 --> 00:05:52.441
A Foundry skill is a SKILL.md file: a short header and Markdown instructions, stored in the project, with immutable versions you can roll back.

81
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:57.302
Mine are Python packages: a graph, its prompts, checks and tests.

82
00:05:57.302 --> 00:06:00.630
A Foundry skill changes what the model is told.

83
00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.392
Mine decides the steps the agent takes.

84
00:06:03.392 --> 00:06:06.719
A Foundry skill is closer to a reusable prompt.

85
00:06:06.719 --> 00:06:09.127
Mine is closer to a small program.

86
00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:11.522
So where do they meet?

87
00:06:11.522 --> 00:06:13.340
In the wording the business owns.

88
00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.323
My prompts hold three kinds of text.

89
00:06:15.576 --> 00:06:21.447
Standards the business owns: how Contoso writes requirements, user stories, and plain English.

90
00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:25.069
Those moved into three Foundry skills, each with an owner.

91
00:06:25.326 --> 00:06:35.865
Rules the code checks stayed in code: the source excerpt must be verbatim, untrusted text is only data, claims are cited, and nothing harmful becomes a requirement.

92
00:06:35.865 --> 00:06:41.134
In a Foundry skill, anyone with project access could weaken them without a review.

93
00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:43.861
In a prompt, it's one line.

94
00:06:43.861 --> 00:06:50.045
The standard comes first, the checked rules come after it, and the wrapper tells the model those rules win.

95
00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:02.470
In Foundry, under Tools, Skills: the three standards, at version 1.

96
00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:06.059
Open one, and there's the text a functional lead can own.

97
00:07:17.512 --> 00:07:21.496
Agent Framework agents let the model decide when to load a skill.

98
00:07:21.496 --> 00:07:28.178
My graph already knows which step needs which standard, so it loads it directly, through the same Skills API.

99
00:07:28.432 --> 00:07:32.857
Production pins the version, so behaviour only changes on a deploy.

100
00:07:32.857 --> 00:07:40.848
Downloaded text is checked, there's a bundled copy if Foundry is unreachable, and every load is audited with its version.

101
00:07:41.952 --> 00:07:43.085
Then it went live.

102
00:07:43.085 --> 00:07:45.794
Three skills published, 9 hosts redeployed.

103
00:07:45.794 --> 00:07:52.533
And in the traces, each prompt carries its standard at version 1, straight from Foundry, with no fallbacks.

104
00:07:53.866 --> 00:07:59.069
Last time, I said Foundry had built-in continuous improvement that I hadn't tried.

105
00:07:59.069 --> 00:07:59.767
Now I have.

106
00:07:59.767 --> 00:08:01.290
The loop has two halves.

107
00:08:02.823 --> 00:08:04.359
Before release, my gate.

108
00:08:04.359 --> 00:08:09.671
Test cases with exact checks, and red teaming with attacks hidden inside documents.

109
00:08:09.671 --> 00:08:11.335
Those found the real bugs.

110
00:08:11.588 --> 00:08:13.736
After release, Foundry watches.

111
00:08:13.736 --> 00:08:18.657
Every hour, it samples recent traces and scores them with judge models.

112
00:08:18.657 --> 00:08:23.854
The built-in evaluators cover task adherence, intent resolution and safety.

113
00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:27.692
On the agent's Evaluation tab, those are the recurring runs.

114
00:08:50.072 --> 00:08:54.488
And the scores land on the Monitor tab, next to runs, tokens and cost.

115
00:08:57.935 --> 00:09:01.851
But the built-in judges know generic quality, not Contoso's.

116
00:09:01.851 --> 00:09:10.468
So the standard the agent is told to follow, the Foundry skill, is now also the standard it's scored against, as a rubric evaluator.

117
00:09:10.468 --> 00:09:14.255
And the rules my code checks became code-based evaluators.

118
00:09:24.322 --> 00:09:32.492
In Foundry, under Evaluations, the Evaluator catalog: Microsoft's built-in evaluators, and five of mine, marked custom.

119
00:09:35.631 --> 00:09:37.314
Open requirements quality.

120
00:09:37.314 --> 00:09:43.206
It's a rubric: eight dimensions, each with a weight, that a judge model scores from 1 to 5.

121
00:09:43.206 --> 00:09:45.084
Keep the scope of the source.

122
00:09:45.084 --> 00:09:46.703
One requirement per need.

123
00:09:46.703 --> 00:09:50.394
Flag vague terms, with a question for the business owner.

124
00:09:53.129 --> 00:09:55.647
Two of mine are code-based, in preview.

125
00:09:55.647 --> 00:09:57.455
No judge model, just Python.

126
00:09:57.455 --> 00:10:07.401
They're the exact checks from my release gate: excerpts must be quoted word for word, and an interface document can't invent an endpoint or a status code.

127
00:10:25.776 --> 00:10:29.378
The hourly rule runs them next to the built-in evaluators.

128
00:10:29.378 --> 00:10:32.793
Each rubric comes back with a pass or fail and a score.

129
00:10:32.793 --> 00:10:39.003
And for every dimension, a score and a written reason, which the built-in evaluators don't give you.

130
00:11:06.501 --> 00:11:09.192
Every result is attached to its trace, too.

131
00:11:09.192 --> 00:11:15.824
Open one, and there's the host, the router and the skill's steps, with the evaluation results beside them.

132
00:11:20.262 --> 00:11:21.845
And that closes the loop.

133
00:11:21.845 --> 00:11:24.062
A low score points at a real trace.

134
00:11:24.062 --> 00:11:30.331
That trace becomes a new golden case, and the fix goes back through my gate before it's redeployed.

135
00:11:31.612 --> 00:11:33.889
Before the takeaways, a quick aside.

136
00:11:33.889 --> 00:11:36.672
Decision models are the hot topic right now.

137
00:11:36.672 --> 00:11:40.847
Microsoft's Decision-1.1 just went generally available in Foundry.

138
00:11:40.847 --> 00:11:46.097
It does one job: it picks from the options you allow, and tells you how sure it is.

139
00:11:46.097 --> 00:11:53.308
And the claims are bold: much faster and much cheaper than a general model, for routing, classifying and checking.

140
00:11:58.808 --> 00:12:02.582
It's already deployed in my project, next to the agents' model.

141
00:12:02.582 --> 00:12:04.379
And it has its own playground.

142
00:12:04.379 --> 00:12:07.854
You give it the evidence, and the decisions you want made.

143
00:12:07.854 --> 00:12:09.771
It gives you back probabilities.

144
00:12:09.771 --> 00:12:10.669
No text at all.

145
00:12:10.982 --> 00:12:13.441
Here's a real case from my link judge.

146
00:12:13.441 --> 00:12:17.387
The requirement: email the applicant when the status changes.

147
00:12:17.387 --> 00:12:20.622
The candidate: an email template branding refresh.

148
00:12:20.622 --> 00:12:22.628
Same word, different behaviour.

149
00:12:22.628 --> 00:12:24.827
Does it implement the requirement?

150
00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:28.728
A low probability.

151
00:12:28.728 --> 00:12:29.810
Correctly, a no.

152
00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:35.019
Now swap in the story that really does email the applicant, and run it again.

153
00:12:39.724 --> 00:12:40.854
Close to certain.

154
00:12:40.854 --> 00:12:43.713
A couple of output tokens, back in seconds.

155
00:12:43.713 --> 00:12:45.242
No essay, no reasoning.

156
00:12:45.242 --> 00:12:47.169
Just a number you can act on.

157
00:12:49.221 --> 00:12:50.968
So where exactly did I try it?

158
00:12:50.968 --> 00:12:52.598
Inside two real agent flows.

159
00:12:52.598 --> 00:13:00.573
In the backlog agent, the requirements traceability skill has one step that asks a model: does this work item implement this requirement?

160
00:13:00.573 --> 00:13:07.734
In the evidence job, the CAE evidence skill has one step that sorts each artifact into direct, supporting, or not evidence.

161
00:13:07.734 --> 00:13:09.655
I swapped only that step's model.

162
00:13:09.655 --> 00:13:15.418
Everything around it stayed the same: the inputs, the code checks after it, and the human approval.

163
00:13:15.718 --> 00:13:18.083
And there's a third contender.

164
00:13:18.083 --> 00:13:24.940
gpt-6-luna is in my project too, and I'd heard it's cheaper and faster than gpt-5-mini.

165
00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:28.881
So, three models: today's, Luna, and Decision-1.1.

166
00:13:29.987 --> 00:13:33.291
The first surprise came before a single result.

167
00:13:33.291 --> 00:13:35.611
Luna refused my agents' settings.

168
00:13:35.611 --> 00:13:40.953
Every agent asks for temperature 0, so the same input gives the same answer.

169
00:13:40.953 --> 00:13:42.359
Luna only accepts 1.

170
00:13:42.359 --> 00:13:45.100
Swap it in blind, and every call fails.

171
00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:48.334
To test it at all, I ran it at 1, three times.

172
00:13:49.976 --> 00:13:54.646
Then the race, in real time: the median per requirement.

173
00:13:54.646 --> 00:13:57.982
Decision-1.1 is done in about 2 seconds.

174
00:13:57.982 --> 00:13:59.316
Luna in under 7.

175
00:13:59.316 --> 00:14:00.984
gpt-5-mini takes 12.

176
00:14:00.984 --> 00:14:07.405
And the output tokens: under a hundred, against 9,000, against almost 20,000.

177
00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:10.010
Now accuracy.

178
00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:14.537
On links, Luna was the best, 96 to 98%, with no traps.

179
00:14:14.537 --> 00:14:20.406
Decision-1.1 tied gpt-5-mini at 94%, but missed three partial matches.

180
00:14:20.406 --> 00:14:24.095
On security evidence, gpt-5-mini won at 93%.

181
00:14:24.095 --> 00:14:25.688
Luna got 90 to 92%.

182
00:14:25.688 --> 00:14:31.640
And Decision-1.1 twice called something direct evidence when it wasn't.

183
00:14:31.898 --> 00:14:32.712
Two twists.

184
00:14:32.712 --> 00:14:36.484
Decision-1.1 flagged 6 of its 7 mistakes as unsure.

185
00:14:36.484 --> 00:14:40.773
Both chat models were confident even when they were wrong.

186
00:14:40.773 --> 00:14:47.430
And combining them, Decision-1.1 first, and only its unsure answers to Luna, averaged 93%.

187
00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:52.015
A tie with today's model, with one chat call instead of three.

188
00:14:53.328 --> 00:14:55.299
So what changed in production?

189
00:14:55.299 --> 00:14:56.152
Nothing, yet.

190
00:14:56.152 --> 00:14:58.845
A cheaper model that rejects my settings.

191
00:14:58.845 --> 00:15:02.064
A faster one that isn't more accurate on its own.

192
00:15:02.064 --> 00:15:04.231
And a combination that only ties.

193
00:15:04.231 --> 00:15:07.121
The hype is real, and so are the benchmarks.

194
00:15:07.121 --> 00:15:10.865
But on my agents and my data, the answers were different.

195
00:15:10.865 --> 00:15:11.653
Always test.

196
00:15:11.653 --> 00:15:12.507
Don't assume.

197
00:15:15.255 --> 00:15:17.140
So, what would I do differently?

198
00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:20.792
Decide the deployment shape separately from the use case list.

199
00:15:20.792 --> 00:15:23.148
Group by trust boundary, not by feature.

200
00:15:23.404 --> 00:15:27.118
Keep every use case traceable, as a skill with its own tests.

201
00:15:27.118 --> 00:15:33.328
Put the wording the business owns in Foundry skills, and score live traffic against that same wording.

202
00:15:33.328 --> 00:15:35.764
Keep the rules your code checks in code.

203
00:15:35.764 --> 00:15:39.052
And add Foundry's monitoring to your own release gate.

204
00:15:39.052 --> 00:15:40.087
Don't replace it.

205
00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:42.631
23 use cases.

206
00:15:42.631 --> 00:15:43.566
9 agents.

207
00:15:43.566 --> 00:15:44.501
One loop.
